  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on a small, qualitative sample of 16 individuals in a single European city, which limits direct statistical generalization to other urban contexts.",
      "what": "Through semi-structured interviews with 16 social housing tenants aged 54 to 83 in Ghent, Belgium, this study examines how financial, health, cognitive, and emotional barriers lead to suppressed travel. The research documents how declining public transport services, driving stress, and planning anxiety cause vulnerable older adults to forgo desired trips, particularly for leisure and family visits outside the city.",
      "why": "The study demonstrates that high urban density and physical proximity to services are insufficient to prevent unmet travel needs, challenging the assumption that dense urban environments automatically resolve transport disadvantage for vulnerable populations."
    }
